<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stevan Šupljikac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stevan Šupljikac was a Serbian officer in the Austrian army who was elected vojvoda, or duke, of the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> by the May Assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a> in 1848. He led the Serb forces against the Hungarian revolutionary government for a short time. He died in December 1848, soon after taking command.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stevan-supljikac.jpg" alt="Stevan Šupljikac" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anastas Jovanović · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Supljikac_color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stevan Šupljikac was born in Petrinja in 1786. He had a brother, Jovan, who was also a fighter, and a sister, Anka, who later married Gabriel Miletić. He entered the Austrian army in 1805 and later rose to the rank of general. Between 1806 and 1814 he served as an officer of the Imperial French army, and during the Russian Campaign of 1812 he was awarded the Légion d'honneur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in Austrian service from 1814, he commanded the Ogulin regiment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lika">Lika</a> and then served as a brigade commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz">Joseph Radetzky von Radetz</a>, receiving the Great Cross of the Iron Crown. In 1848 he took part in the suppression of Italian rebels during the Unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> under Austrian rule demanded the recognition of Serbian as an official language, equality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church with the Catholic, and annual church assemblies. Several thousand Serbs met at the May Assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a> on 1 May 1848, which chose Šupljikac as voivode, the civil and military commander, and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josif-rajacic">Josif Rajačić</a> patriarch. They demanded a national unit made up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranja">Baranja</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srem</a>, known together as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fighting between Hungarian and Serbian bands began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> in June. A large wave of volunteers came from the Principality in June and July, and Stevan Knićanin, elected military commander, arrived on 25 July. While the Hungarians were hostile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>: the new emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> approved the establishment of the Serbian Vojvodina with Šupljikac as its duke, its first voivode. On 6 October he became supreme military commander of the Serbian national troops. He died at Pančevo on 15 December 1848 and was buried in the Krušedol Monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stevan Šupljikac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A national assembly at Sremski Karlovci in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian Vojvodina in Srem, Baranya, Bačka and Banat, elected Josif Rajačić patriarch and Colonel <b>Stevan Šupljikac</b> voivode, and set up a Main Board led by Đorđe Stratimirović.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Petrinja, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in Pančevo, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,16.29&amp;year=1826" aria-label="Petrinja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stevan Šupljikac"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4185 6147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4185 6147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,16.29&amp;year=1826">What was here in 1826? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
